ection polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Michael Baumgartner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Washington's fifth district leans Republican, and first-term incumbent Michael Baumgartner enters the general election with clear structural advantages. He has raised roughly $2.2 million to Carmela Conroy's $840,000 and holds a large cash edge, while outside spending against him has been modest. The two are meeting in a 2024 rematch, with debates centered on affordability, trade, Medicaid, and data centers.\n\nRatings from across the spectrum place the seat in Safe or Likely Republican territory, and prediction markets give Conroy roughly a one-in-six chance of winning. The only publicly available polls, conducted for Democratic sponsors in the spring, showed Baumgartner ahead by about six points. A favorable national climate for Democrats this year is the main reason the race is not rated even more firmly Republican; a wave-scale shift late in the campaign would be needed to put it in play.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0968, "margin": -8.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1087, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0951, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0945, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1116, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0732, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0882, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1005, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.124, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1258, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1286, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.127, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.1239, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.1055, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.105, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.0723, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.1039,